
  Before approaches, I need to pin down what's being stored, because two different subsystems are hiding under "track
  who logged in" and they have different shapes.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether you need [3mcurrent identity[0m (any part of the app can ask "who is logged in right now?") or an [3mevent [0m
  [3mhistory[0m (a record of each login that happened, readable later). They're not the same object and they don't have the
  same lifetime.

  - [1mCurrent identity / session store[0m — one live value, overwritten at each login, cleared at logout. Other forms read it
    to stamp their submissions. This is what "other forms will need it later" sounds like to me. Small; the hard parts
    are storage medium and what happens on page reload.
  - [1mLogin event log[0m — an append-only list of [38;5;153m{who, when, outcome}[39m. Answers "who logged in" historically, for audit or
    analytics. Other forms don't read it; they write to it. Needs a destination (server, or it's lost on refresh) and a
    retention answer.
  - [1mBoth[0m — a session store that also emits an event per login. Real, but it's two designs; I'd build identity first and
    add events on top rather than tangle them.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m current identity, with event logging deferred unless you need audit now. "Other forms will need it"
  is a read pattern, and only the session store serves reads. If you say both, I'd still sequence identity first — it's
  the dependency, and the event log is cheap to add afterward. Getting this wrong is expensive: the storage medium and
  the shape of the stored value are the two things later callers bind to hardest.
